--- PART A: the broker's bounds on what a consumer may ask for ---
$ docker exec maestro-demo-kafka-1 kafka-configs.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
    --entity-type brokers --entity-name 1 --describe --all | grep session.timeout
      group.max.session.timeout.ms=1800000 sensitive=false synonyms={DEFAULT_CONFIG:group.max.session.timeout.ms=1800000}
      group.min.session.timeout.ms=6000 sensitive=false synonyms={DEFAULT_CONFIG:group.min.session.timeout.ms=6000}

    => the classic-protocol bounds are 6 s..1800 s, so a consumer asking for 45 s
       gets 45 s verbatim; the broker does not clamp it.

--- PART B: what the demo's consumers actually negotiated (effective ConsumerConfig) ---
Kafka logs the resolved ConsumerConfig at consumer construction. Taken from the
QA run's own service logs in demo/.run/ (untracked; quoted here so the figure is archived):
    loan-application-service         session.timeout.ms = 45000
    verification-gateway-service     session.timeout.ms = 45000
    underwriting-service             session.timeout.ms = 45000
    maestro-admin                    session.timeout.ms = 45000
    loan-application-service-b       session.timeout.ms = 45000

    All five JVMs: session.timeout.ms = 45000 (45 s). Nothing in the demo or the
    sample overrides it — it is Spring Kafka passing through the Apache default,
    and this broker accepts it unchanged.

--- VERDICT ---
RUNBOOK §2's '45 s by default' is correct FOR THIS BROKER AND THESE CONSUMERS, not
merely correct in the abstract. It bounds the measured 37.2 s post-SIGKILL rejoin in
demo/.evidence/task-6-kafka-rejoin-measured.log:28-33 (37.2 < 45), which is the
arithmetic the runbook now states.
